vered square and each column contains one uncovered square, the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\\[\nU=\\{\\,(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(|U|=n\\) and the remaining \\(n^{2}-n\\) squares must be tiled.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound \u2013 a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nChoose the permutation so that \\(\\sigma(1)=1\\) and \\(\\sigma(n)=n\\); i.e., the uncovered squares in the first and last rows lie at the extreme columns.  The other \\(n-2\\) uncovered squares may be placed arbitrarily.\n\nConsider a column \\(j\\).  Let \\(i\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=j\\) (the hole in column \\(j\\)).\n* If \\(i=1\\) or \\(i=n\\) (the hole is on the top or bottom border), the column can be covered by a single vertical rectangle of size \\( (n-1)\\times 1\\) \u2013 either rows \\(2\\dots n\\) or rows \\(1\\dots n-1\\).\n* If \\(1<i<n\\) (the hole is interior), the column must be split into two vertical rectangles:\n  * one covering rows \\(1\\dots i-1\\),\n  * another covering rows \\(i+1\\dots n\\).\n  Both rectangles have width \\(1\\) and heights \\(i-1\\) and \\(n-i\\) respectively.\n\nAll columns are disjoint, so the rectangles do not overlap.  Counting them gives\n\\[\n\\text{#tiles}=\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{columns of rows 1 and n}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{2\\,(n-2)}_{\\text{interior columns}}\\;=\\;2n-2.\n\\]\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound \u2013 at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nLet any admissible tiling be given.  Examine a column \\(j\\) whose uncovered square lies in an interior row \\(i\\) (so \\(1<i<n\\)).\nThe squares of column \\(j\\) are split into two non\u2011empty sets:\n\\[A=\\{(r,j)\\mid 1\\le r<i\\},\\qquad B=\\{(r,j)\\mid i<r\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nSuppose, for contradiction, that a single rectangle \\(R\\) contains a square from \\(A\\) and a square from \\(B\\).  Because \\(R\\) is axis\u2011parallel, its row interval must contain the whole range \\([1,n]\\); consequently it also contains the cell \\((i,j)\\), which is uncovered.  This contradicts the requirement that no tile may cover an uncovered square.  Hence the squares of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to **different** tiles.\nTherefore every column whose hole is interior contributes *at least* two distinct tiles.\n\nOnly the columns containing the holes of the first row (row \\(1\\)) and the last row (row \\(n\\)) can avoid this forced split, because in those columns the hole lies on the boundary of the grid; the column can then be covered by a single rectangle that omits the boundary row.\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) columns and at most two of them (the ones with holes in rows \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)) can be covered by a single tile, the total number of tiles satisfies\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-2)+1+1\\;=\\;2n-2.\n\\]\nThus any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Optimality\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound argument of Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2\\;=\\;2\\cdot2025-2\\;=\\;4048.\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n",
