{
    "solution": "### Summary\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- Model the uncovered squares by a permutation $p$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ where $n=2025$; the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $p(i)$.  \n- Split the board into the *left* part (cells with column $<p(i)$) and the *right* part (cells with column $>p(i)$).  \n- Show that any rectangle covering cells of the left part must be anchored at the left border; its rightmost column is a well\u2011defined number $c$.  \n- For each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ there is a unique row $i_k$ with $p(i_k)=k+1$ (so its left part has exactly $k$ columns).  The cell $(i_k,k)$ must be covered, and any rectangle covering it must have rightmost column exactly $k$.  Hence at least $n-1$ left\u2011anchored rectangles are needed.\n- By symmetry the same argument gives at least $n-1$ right\u2011anchored rectangles.\n- Therefore any tiling uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\n- Construct a tiling attaining this bound: take the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  For each column $j$ ($1\\le j\\le n-1$) place a vertical $1\\times (n-j)$ rectangle covering rows $j+1,\\dots,n$ in column $j$; for each column $j$ ($2\\le j\\le n$) place a vertical $1\\times (j-1)$ rectangle covering rows $1,\\dots,j-1$ in column $j$.  This yields $n-1$ rectangles below the diagonal and $n-1$ above it, altogether $2n-2$ tiles, and they cover every cell except the diagonal.\n- Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet $n=2025$.  In each row $i$ ($1\\le i\\le n$) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by $p(i)$.  The map $p:\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ is a permutation, because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nFor a row $i$ define\n\\[\nL_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad R_i:=n-p(i).\n\\]\nThus $L_i$ is the number of covered squares to the left of the uncovered one in row $i$, and $R_i$ is the number to its right.  Note that the multiset $\\{L_i\\}$ is $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ (since $p$ is a permutation), and similarly $\\{R_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**1.  A lower bound for the left side**\n\nConsider only the cells with column $<p(i)$ (the *left* part).  Any rectangle $\\mathcal R$ that contains such a cell cannot intersect the uncovered square of any row it uses; consequently, for every row $i$ belonging to $\\mathcal R$ the whole column interval of $\\mathcal R$ must be contained in $\\{1,\\dots,p(i)-1\\}$.  Hence the rightmost column of $\\mathcal R$ is a well\u2011defined integer $c$, and we must have $c< p(i)$ for all rows $i$ of $\\mathcal R$.  In other words, $\\mathcal R$ is *left\u2011anchored* and its rightmost column is $c$.\n\nFor each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ there is a unique row $i_k$ with $L_{i_k}=k$, i.e. $p(i_k)=k+1$.  The cell $(i_k,k)$ lies in the left part and must be covered.  Any left\u2011anchored rectangle covering $(i_k,k)$ must have rightmost column $c\\ge k$, but it cannot have $c\\ge k+1$ because column $k+1$ is uncovered in row $i_k$.  Therefore $c=k$.\n\nConsequently, the $n-1$ distinguished cells $(i_k,k)$ ($k=1,\\dots,n-1$) require $n-1$ distinct rectangles, because two different values of $k$ force two different rightmost columns.  Hence any tiling needs at least $n-1$ rectangles to cover the left part.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**2.  A lower bound for the right side**\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  For the *right* part (cells with column $>p(i)$) any rectangle is *right\u2011anchored*: its leftmost column $c$ satisfies $c>p(i)$ for every row $i$ it contains.  For each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ there is a unique row $j_k$ with $R_{j_k}=k$, i.e. $p(j_k)=n-k$.  The cell $(j_k,n-k+1)$ belongs to the right part and any right\u2011anchored rectangle covering it must have leftmost column exactly $n-k+1$.  Thus the $n-1$ cells $(j_k,n-k+1)$ require $n-1$ distinct rectangles.\n\nTherefore at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed for the right part as well.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**3.  Global lower bound**\n\nEvery tile is either left\u2011anchored or right\u2011anchored (it cannot cross the uncovered square of a row it occupies).  Hence the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\n\\[\nT\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**4.  A construction attaining the bound**\n\nChoose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$; the uncovered squares are the main diagonal $(i,i)$.  For each column $j$ with $1\\le j\\le n-1$ place a vertical rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):\\;j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares below the diagonal in column $j$.  For each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n$ place a vertical rectangle\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):\\;1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares above the diagonal in column $j$.  Each of these rectangles is a $1\\times (n-j)$ or $1\\times (j-1)$ tile; they are pairwise disjoint and together they cover every unit square except the $n$ diagonal squares.  The number of tiles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this gives $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$ tiles.\n\nSince the lower bound $2n-2$ is achieved, it is optimal.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\nThus the answer is 4048.\n",
