lemma to $L$, where $k=n-1$, we obtain that any tiling of $L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The same argument applied to $R$ (where the row lengths are $n-\\sigma(i)$, again the set $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$) shows that at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed for $R$ as well.\n\nTherefore **any** tiling of the whole board requires at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\nTake the identity permutation $\\sigma(i)=i$ (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  Then\n\\[\nL = \\{(i,j):j<i\\},\\qquad R = \\{(i,j):j>i\\}.\n\\]\nTile $L$ by the $n-1$ vertical rectangles\n\\[\nV_k = \\{(i,k): i=k+1,\\dots,n\\},\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots,n-1.\n\\]\nEach $V_k$ has size $(n-k)\\times1$ and the $V_k$ are disjoint and together cover all of $L$.\n\nTile $R$ by the $n-1$ horizontal rectangles\n\\[\nH_k = \\{(k,j): j=k+1,\\dots,n\\},\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots,n-1.\n\\]\nEach $H_k$ has size $1\\times(n-k)$, they are disjoint, and they cover all of $R$.\n\nThus we have used exactly $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles, and every row and every column contains precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  Hence the lower bound is tight.\n\n---\n### 4.  Answer for $n=2025$\nPlugging $n=2025$ into $2n-2$ gives\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is **4048**.\n\n---\n**Conclusion.**  For a $2025\\times2025$ grid the smallest number of rectangular tiles that can satisfy the condition is $\\boxed{4048}$.\n",
