            "role": "assistant",
            "content": "{\n  \"solution\": \"Let the grid have size $n\\\\times n$ with $n=2025$.  The uncovered squares must be one in each row and one in each column, so they form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that the uncovered squares are $\\\\{(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  All other unit squares have to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles, no two tiles overlapping.\\n\\n---\\n### 1.  Decomposition of the board\\nFor a row $i$ define\\n\\\\[\\nL_i=\\\\{(i,j):1\\\\le j<\\\\sigma(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad R_i=\\\\{(i,j):\\\\sigma(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus $L_i$ is the set of squares to the *left* of the hole in row $i$, and $R_i$ the set to the *right* of it.  The whole set of squares to be covered is the disjoint union\\n\\\\[\\nL:=\\\\bigcup_{i=1}^n L_i,\\\\qquad R:=\\\\bigcup_{i=1}^n R_i.\\n\\\\]\\nA rectangle cannot contain squares from both $L$ and $R$, because any horizontal line through a rectangle would have to cross the hole of the row it meets.  Consequently every tile lies entirely in $L$ or entirely in $R$.\\n\\n---\\n### 2.  A lower bound for $L$\\nRow $i$ contains exactly $|L_i|=\\\\sigma(i)-1$ squares of $L$.  Since $\\\\sigma$ is a permutation, the multiset $\\\\{\\\\sigma(i)-1\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ is precisely $\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$.  Hence $L$ has $n-1$ distinct positive row lengths.\\n\\n**Lemma.**  If a Ferrers (Young) diagram has $k$ distinct positive row lengths, any partition of it into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least $k$ rectangles.\\n\\n*Proof of the lemma.*  Consider a rectangle $R$ that occupies rows $i_1<i_2<\\\\dots<i_t$ of the diagram.  Because the diagram is left‑justified, the width of $R$ cannot exceed the smallest row length among those rows; otherwise $R$ would contain a hole.  Thus the width of $R$ equals $\\\\min\\\\{\\\\sigma(i_j)-1\\\\}$.  Two different positive row lengths cannot be realised by the same rectangle, so each distinct length forces a distinct rectangle.  Hence at least $k$ rectangles are needed.  ∎\\n\\nApplying the lemma to $L$, where $k=n-1$, we obtain that any tiling of $L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The same argument applied to $R$ (where the row lengths are $n-\\\\sigma(i)$, again the set $\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$) shows that at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed for $R$ as well.\\n\\nTherefore **any** tiling of the whole board requires at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n---\\n### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\\nTake the identity permutation $\\\\sigma(i)=i$ (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  Then\\n\\\\[\\nL = \\\\{(i,j):j<i\\\\},\\\\qquad R = \\\\{(i,j):j>i\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nTile $L$ by the $n-1$ vertical rectangles\\n\\\\[\\nV_k = \\\\{(i,k): i=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\qquad k=1,2,\\\\dots,n-1.\\n\\\\]\\nEach $V_k$ has size $(n-k)\\\\times1$ and the $V_k$ are disjoint and together cover all of $L$.\\n\\nTile $R$ by the $n-1$ horizontal rectangles\\n\\\\[\\nH_k = \\\\{(k,j): j=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\qquad k=1,2,\\\\dots,n-1.\\n\\\\]\\nEach $H_k$ has size $1\\\\times(n-k)$, they are disjoint, and they cover all of $R$.\\n\\nThus we have used exactly $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles, and every row and every column contains precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  Hence the lower bound is tight.\\n\\n---\\n### 4.  Answer for $n=2025$\\nPlugging $n=2025$ into $2n-2$ gives\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is **4048**.\\n\\n---\\n**Conclusion.**  For a $2025\\\\times2025$ grid the smallest number of rectangular tiles that can satisfy the condition is $\\\\boxed{4048}$.\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
