e \\\\(n-1\\\\) horizontal rectangles \\\\(H_k=\\\\{(k,j)\\\\mid j=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\) for \\\\(k=1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\).\\n   - These rectangles are disjoint, cover every square except the diagonal, and each row and column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).\\n5. Counting the tiles gives \\\\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\).  For \\\\(n=2025\\\\) this equals \\\\(4048\\\\).\\n\\nThus the lower bound is tight and the minimum number of tiles is \\\\(4048\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet \\\\(n=2025\\\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\\\(1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\).  A unit square is denoted by its coordinate pair \\\\((i,j)\\\\) where \\\\(i\\\\) is the row and \\\\(j\\\\) the column.\\n\\n#### 1.  Description of the uncovered squares\\nSince each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a function\\n\\\\[\\\\sigma:\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\to\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\]\\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\\\(i\\\\) is \\\\((i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\).  The condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square forces \\\\(\\\\sigma\\\\) to be a bijection; hence \\\\(\\\\sigma\\\\) is a permutation of \\\\(\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\).\\n\\n#### 2.  The left and right regions\\nDefine\\n\\\\[L = \\\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<\\\\sigma(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad\\n   R = \\\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\sigma(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(L\\\\) consists of all squares strictly to the left of the uncovered square in their row, and \\\\(R\\\\) consists of all squares strictly to the right.  The set of squares that must be covered is exactly \\\\(L\\\\cup R\\\\); the uncovered squares themselves are not to be covered.\\n\\n#### 3.  Every tile lies entirely in \\\\(L\\\\) or entirely in \\\\(R\\\\)\\nConsider a rectangular tile \\\\(T\\\\) whose sides lie on grid lines.  Suppose \\\\(T\\\\) occupies rows \\\\(i_1<i_2<\\\\dots<i_t\\\\) and columns \\\\(c_1<c_2<\\\\dots<c_s\\\\).  For each occupied row \\\\(i_k\\\\) the uncovered column is \\\\(\\\\sigma(i_k)\\\\).  Because \\\\(T\\\\) may not contain the uncovered square, we must have either\\n\\\\[c_s < \\\\sigma(i_k)\\\\quad\\\\text{for all }k\\\\]\\nor\\n\\\\[c_1 > \\\\sigma(i_k)\\\\quad\\\\text{for all }k.\\\\]\\nIf for some rows the uncovered column lies to the left of \\\\(T\\\\) and for other rows to the right, the two inequalities would contradict the fact that \\\\(c_1\\\\le c_s\\\\).  Hence the same inequality holds for every occupied row.  Consequently \\\\(T\\\\) is completely contained in \\\\(L\\\\) (the first case) or completely contained in \\\\(R\\\\) (the second case).\\n\\nThus the collection of tiles splits into two sub\u2011collections: those covering \\\\(L\\\\) and those covering \\\\(R\\\\).\\n\\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the number of tiles covering \\\\(L\\\\)\\nFor each row \\\\(i\\\\) the number of squares of \\\\(L\\\\) in that row is\\n\\\\[\\\\ell_i = \\\\sigma(i)-1.\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(\\\\sigma\\\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\\\(\\\\{\\\\ell_i\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\) is exactly \\\\(\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}\\\\).  Hence there are \\\\(n-1\\\\) distinct positive values among the \\\\(\\\\ell_i\\\\).\\n\\nNow look at the columns of \\\\(L\\\\).  For a column index \\\\(j\\\\) with \\\\(1\\\\le j\\\\le n-1\\\\) define\\n\\\\[C_j = \\\\{i\\\\mid \\\\ell_i \\\\ge j\\\\} = \\\\{i\\\\mid \\\\sigma(i) > j\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe set \\\\(C_j\\\\) consists of the rows that contain a square in column \\\\(j\\\\).  Its cardinality is\\n\\\\[|C_j| = \\\\#\\\\{i\\\\mid \\\\sigma(i) > j\\\\} = n-j,\\\\]\\nbecause exactly \\\\(j\\\\) values of the permutation are \\\\(\\\\le j\\\\).  Thus the column heights \\\\(|C_j|\\\\) for \\\\(j=1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\) are the distinct numbers \\\\(n-1,n-2,\\\\dots,1\\\\).\\n\\nConsider any rectangle \\\\(T\\\\) that lies in \\\\(L\\\\).  Let its leftmost column be \\\\(a\\\\) and its rightmost column be \\\\(b\\\\) (so the width is \\\\(b-a+1\\\\)).  Since \\\\(L\\\\) is left\u2011justified, the only possible leftmost column is \\\\(a=1\\\\); otherwise column \\\\(a-1\\\\) would be missing from some row that contains column \\\\(a\\\\), contradicting the rectangular shape.  Hence every rectangle in \\\\(L\\\\) is of the form\\n\\\\[\\\\{(i,1),\\\\dots,(i,b)\\\\}\\\\text{ for a set of rows }i.\\\\]\\nConsequently the width of \\\\(T\\\\) equals some integer \\\\(b\\\\) with \\\\(1\\\\le b\\\\le n-1\\\\).\\n\\nIf a rectangle has width \\\\(b\\\\), it can cover squares only in columns \\\\(1,2,\\\\dots,b\\\\).  In particular, it can cover squares in column \\\\(b\\\\) but cannot cover any square in column \\\\(b+1\\\\).  Therefore a rectangle of width \\\\(b\\\\) is the *only* type of rectangle that can cover the squares of column \\\\(b\\\\) that lie in rows where the row length is exactly \\\\(b\\\\).  Since column \\\\(b\\\\) contains a square belonging to a row with \\\\(\\\\ell_i = b\\\\) (by the permutation property), that square must be covered by a rectangle whose width is exactly \\\\(b\\\\).\\n\\nThus for each \\\\(b\\\\in\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}\\\\) there must exist at least one rectangle of width \\\\(b\\\\) in any tiling of \\\\(L\\\\).  Rectangles of different widths are distinct, so at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) rectangles are required to cover \\\\(L\\\\).\\n\\n#### 5.  The same bound for \\\\(R\\\\)\\nThe region \\\\(R\\\\) is symmetric: after reflecting the board horizontally, \\\\(R\\\\) becomes a left\u2011justified shape whose row lengths are \\\\(r_i = n-\\\\sigma(i)\\\\).  The multiset \\\\(\\\\{r_i\\\\}\\\\) is again \\\\(\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}\\\\), and the argument of the previous section applies verbatim.  Hence any tiling of \\\\(R\\\\) also needs at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) rectangles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Global lower bound\\nSince the tiles covering \\\\(L\\\\) and those covering \\\\(R\\\\) are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling is at least\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\\\]\\nFor \\\\(n=2025\\\\) this gives a lower bound of \\\\(4048\\\\).\\n\\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound\\nChoose the identity permutation \\\\(\\\\sigma(i)=i\\\\) for all \\\\(i\\\\).  The uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\\\((i,i)\\\\).\\n\\n*Left region \\\\(L\\\\).*  For each column \\\\(k\\\\) with \\\\(1\\\\le k\\\\le n-1\\\\) define the vertical rectangle\\n\\\\[V_k = \\\\{(i,k)\\\\mid i=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\(V_k\\\\) has height \\\\(n-k\\\\) and width \\\\(1\\\\).  The rectangles \\\\(V_1,\\\\dots,V_{n-1}\\\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square of \\\\(L\\\\) (all squares strictly below the diagonal).\\n\\n*Right region \\\\(R\\\\).*  For each row \\\\(k\\\\) with \\\\(1\\\\le k\\\\le n-1\\\\) define the horizontal rectangle\\n\\\\[H_k = \\\\{(k,j)\\\\mid j=k+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\(H_k\\\\) has height \\\\(1\\\\) and width \\\\(n-k\\\\).  The rectangles \\\\(H_1,\\\\dots,H_{n-1}\\\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square of \\\\(R\\\\) (all squares strictly above the diagonal).\\n\\nThe two families \\\\(\\\\{V_k\\\\}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\{H_k\\\\}\\\\) are disjoint because one consists of vertical strips in columns left of the diagonal and the other consists of horizontal strips in rows above the diagonal.  Altogether we have \\\\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\) rectangles.\\n\\n row \\\\(i\\\\) contains the uncovered square \\\\((i,i)\\\\) and, besides that, the row is covered either by the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_{i-1}\\\\) (if \\\\(i>1\\\\)) or by the horizontal rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) (if \\\\(i<n\\\\)).  Similarly, each column \\\\(j\\\\) contains the uncovered square \\\\((j,j)\\\\) and is otherwise covered by exactly one of the rectangles \\\\(V_j\\\\) or \\\\(H_{j-1}\\\\).  Hence every row and every column has precisely one uncovered unit square, as required.\\n\\nThus a tiling with \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles exists, matching the lower bound.\\n\\n#### 8.  Conclusion\\nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is\\n\\\\[2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\\\]\\nHence the answer is \\\\(4048\\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Answer:** \\\\(\\\\boxed{4048}\\\\)\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
